WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.280
How do you turn an

2
00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:04.340
epsilon nondeterministic automaton into a

3
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.240
deterministic one? Store the entire

4
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.900
active-state set as a single

5
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.560
deterministic state. We will build

6
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.220
the reachable subset table for

7
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.040
the tutorial machine and justify

8
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.980
why it recognizes the same

9
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:17.340
words.

10
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.680
The tutorial machine has states

11
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.760
one, two and three, with

12
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.120
three accepting. On a, one

13
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.660
reaches one and two, two

14
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.580
reaches three, and three reaches

15
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.820
one; on b, only two

16
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.020
has a move, looping to

17
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.820
itself. There is also an

18
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.360
epsilon arrow from two to

19
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:34.680
three.

20
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.080
Start with the epsilon closure

21
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.740
of the original start state.

22
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.280
Here that is just the

23
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.380
singleton containing one, so it

24
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.220
is not accepting. In general

25
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.940
the closure may contain several

26
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.920
states or even an accepting

27
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.940
state, and replacing it blindly

28
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.380
by the singleton start is

29
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:51.780
a mistake.

30
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.780
From the set containing one,

31
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.540
read a. Union all a-targets

32
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.600
to get one and two,

33
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.760
then follow the epsilon arrow

34
00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.580
from two to three. Call

35
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.240
the resulting set containing all

36
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.000
three states B, which is

37
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.780
accepting because it contains three.

38
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.840
From A on b there

39
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.580
are no targets, giving the

40
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.680
empty subset. That is a

41
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.880
real deterministic sink state, with

42
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.780
both symbols returning to itself.

43
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.060
From B, a returns to

44
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.640
B, while b reaches two

45
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.640
and then its epsilon closure

46
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.340
gives C containing two and

47
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:23.680
three.

48
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.980
Now expand C instead of

49
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.340
stopping when the table looks

50
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.780
convincing. On a, states two

51
00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.140
and three reach three and

52
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.260
one, giving D containing one

53
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.180
and three. On b, the

54
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.660
loop at two followed by

55
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.100
closure returns to C.

56
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.940
Finally expand D. Its a-targets

57
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.500
close to B, and it

58
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.660
has no b-target, so b

59
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.740
reaches the empty sink. No

60
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.560
new subsets remain to inspect,

61
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.420
giving five reachable deterministic states

62
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.260
rather than all eight subsets

63
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.480
of a three-element set.

64
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.100
The formal transition rule is

65
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.640
closure of the union of

66
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.620
all symbol-successors. After every input

67
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.160
prefix, the deterministic state equals

68
00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.860
exactly the nondeterministic active set

69
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.520
including free moves. Induction on

70
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.560
prefix length proves this statement,

71
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.700
so containing an old final

72
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.680
state is precisely the correct

73
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.620
accepting condition.

74
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.520
Trace a b through the

75
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.600
new table as a check.

76
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.280
A moves to B on

77
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.820
a and then C on

78
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.360
b, so the word is

79
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.340
accepted. An implementation can construct

80
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.760
only reachable subsets, but the

81
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.340
general upper bound is still

82
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.480
two to the number of

83
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.180
original states.

84
00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:35.860
Things to take note of.

85
00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.480
Compute the initial epsilon closure,

86
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.900
union every active state’s next

87
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.760
moves, and close again after

88
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.840
each consumed symbol. Mark a

89
00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.780
subset accepting when it intersects

90
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.760
the original final set, complete

91
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.720
the empty-sink row, and continue

92
00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.420
until every discovered subset has

93
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.100
been expanded.
